/* Extra flashcards: definitions, formulas, calculations, causes and examples.
   Same format as the spec cards: [topic id, question, answer]. */
window.MORE_CARDS=[
/* ======================= MICRO ======================= */
/* 1.1 The economic problem */
["mi1.1","Define scarcity.","Resources are finite but wants are unlimited, so not every want can be satisfied. This is why choices have to be made."],
["mi1.1","Economic good vs free good?","Economic good: scarce, has an opportunity cost, so it has a price.\nFree good: unlimited supply and zero opportunity cost (air, sea water)."],
["mi1.1","Needs vs wants?","Needs: things essential for survival (food, water, shelter).\nWants: desires beyond needs. They're unlimited."],
["mi1.1","What is a positive statement? Give an example.","An objective statement that can be tested against evidence.\n‘Raising the minimum wage increases unemployment among young workers.’"],
["mi1.1","What is a normative statement? Give an example.","A value judgement that can't be tested. It often contains ‘should’ or ‘ought’.\n‘The government should raise the minimum wage.’"],
["mi1.1","What counts as ‘land’ as a factor of production?","All natural resources: the land itself, minerals, oil, forests, fish stocks."],
["mi1.1","What counts as ‘capital’ as a factor of production?","Man-made goods used to produce other goods: machinery, factories, tools, roads, IT systems."],
["mi1.1","What is enterprise?","The factor that organises land, labour and capital and takes the risk of production. Its reward is profit."],
["mi1.1","What are the three basic economic questions?","What to produce? How to produce it? For whom to produce it?"],
["mi1.1","What does it mean for an agent to act rationally?","To weigh up costs and benefits and choose what maximises their objective: utility for consumers, profit for firms, social welfare for government."],
["mi1.1","What is bounded rationality?","People try to be rational but are limited by incomplete information, limited time and limited ability to process information, so they use rules of thumb."],

/* 1.2 Allocation of resources */
["mi1.2","Define an incentive.","Something that motivates an economic agent to behave in a certain way, e.g. higher profits encouraging firms to supply more."],
["mi1.2","What is a market economy?","Resources are allocated by the price mechanism, with private ownership and a minimal role for the state."],
["mi1.2","What is a planned (command) economy?","The state owns most resources and decides what is produced, how and for whom. Example: the former Soviet Union, North Korea."],
["mi1.2","What is a mixed economy?","Resources are allocated partly by markets and partly by the state, as in the UK (private firms plus the NHS, state schools)."],
["mi1.2","What are the three functions of the price mechanism?","Signalling: prices show where resources are wanted.\nIncentivising: higher prices encourage more supply.\nRationing: higher prices limit demand to what's available."],
["mi1.2","Define productive efficiency.","Producing at the lowest possible average cost. Shown by any point on the PPC, or the bottom of a firm's AC curve (MC = AC)."],
["mi1.2","Define allocative efficiency.","Producing the mix of goods consumers value most, where price = marginal cost (P = MC). Society's welfare is maximised."],
["mi1.2","What is Pareto efficiency?","An allocation where nobody can be made better off without making someone else worse off."],
["mi1.2","Two advantages and two disadvantages of a market economy?","Advantages: efficient allocation through prices; incentives to innovate.\nDisadvantages: market failures (externalities, public goods); inequality."],

/* 1.3 Opportunity cost */
["mi1.3","Define opportunity cost.","The value of the next best alternative given up when making a choice."],
["mi1.3","What does a PPC show?","The maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce when all its resources are fully and efficiently used."],
["mi1.3","What does a point inside the PPC show?","Unemployed or inefficiently used resources. Output of both goods could rise with no opportunity cost."],
["mi1.3","What does a point outside the PPC show?","An output that is unattainable with current resources and technology."],
["mi1.3","Why is a PPC usually concave (bowed out)?","Resources aren't equally good at producing everything, so opportunity cost rises as more of one good is produced."],
["mi1.3","What does a straight-line PPC mean?","Constant opportunity cost: resources are equally suited to producing both goods."],
["mi1.3","What shifts the PPC outwards?","More or better factors of production: investment in capital, new technology, education and training, immigration, discovering new resources."],
["mi1.3","Why might a country choose to produce more capital goods now?","Fewer consumer goods today, but the PPC shifts further out in future: a trade-off between consumption now and growth later."],

/* 2.1 Specialisation and trade */
["mi2.1","Define specialisation.","When individuals, firms, regions or countries concentrate on producing a limited range of goods or services."],
["mi2.1","Define the division of labour.","Breaking production into separate tasks, each done by a different worker."],
["mi2.1","Advantages of the division of labour?","Higher productivity; skills improve through repetition; less time lost switching tasks; specialist machinery can be used; workers do what they're best at."],
["mi2.1","Disadvantages of the division of labour?","Boredom and low motivation; dependency on others (one breakdown halts production); structural unemployment if demand for a narrow skill falls."],
["mi2.1","What is the main problem with barter?","It needs a double coincidence of wants: each party must want exactly what the other is offering."],
["mi2.1","What are the four functions of money?","Medium of exchange; store of value; unit of account; standard of deferred payment."],
["mi2.1","Why does specialisation need money?","Specialists must trade to get everything else they need. Money as a medium of exchange removes the double coincidence of wants that barter requires."],

/* 2.2 Demand */
["mi2.2","Define demand.","The quantity of a good consumers are willing and able to buy at each price over a given period."],
["mi2.2","What is effective demand?","A desire to buy that is backed by the ability to pay."],
["mi2.2","Why does demand slope downwards?","Income effect: a lower price raises real income.\nSubstitution effect: the good is cheaper relative to others.\nDiminishing marginal utility."],
["mi2.2","What causes a shift in demand? (PIRATES)","Population, Income, Related goods (prices of substitutes and complements), Advertising, Tastes, Expectations, Seasons."],
["mi2.2","Define joint demand.","Goods demanded together because they're complements, e.g. cars and petrol, printers and ink."],
["mi2.2","Define competitive demand.","Goods that are substitutes, so they compete for consumers, e.g. Coke and Pepsi."],
["mi2.2","Define composite demand.","When a good is demanded for more than one use, e.g. milk for cheese, butter and yoghurt; steel for cars and construction."],
["mi2.2","Movement along vs shift of demand?","Movement (extension/contraction): caused only by a change in the good's own price.\nShift (increase/decrease): caused by any other factor."],
["mi2.2","What happens to demand for tea if the price of coffee rises?","Demand for tea increases (shifts right), because they're substitutes."],

/* 2.3 Supply */
["mi2.3","Define supply.","The quantity of a good producers are willing and able to sell at each price over a given period."],
["mi2.3","Why does supply slope upwards?","A higher price makes production more profitable, it covers rising marginal costs, and it attracts new firms into the market."],
["mi2.3","What shifts supply?","Costs of production (wages, raw materials, energy), productivity and technology, indirect taxes, subsidies, number of firms, weather, expectations."],
["mi2.3","Define joint supply.","When producing one good automatically produces another: beef and leather, petrol and diesel from crude oil."],
["mi2.3","Define competitive supply.","When the same resources can produce one good or another, e.g. land for wheat or barley; a factory for cars or vans."],
["mi2.3","What happens to supply when an indirect tax is imposed?","Supply shifts left (up) by the amount of the tax, because the tax is a cost of production."],
["mi2.3","What happens to supply when a subsidy is given?","Supply shifts right (down) by the amount of the subsidy, because it lowers firms' costs."],

/* 2.4 Consumer and producer surplus */
["mi2.4","Define consumer surplus.","The difference between what consumers are willing to pay and what they actually pay. The area under the demand curve and above the price."],
["mi2.4","Define producer surplus.","The difference between the price producers receive and the minimum they'd accept. The area above the supply curve and below the price."],
["mi2.4","What happens to consumer and producer surplus when an indirect tax is imposed?","Both fall. Part of the lost surplus becomes tax revenue; the rest is a deadweight loss."],
["mi2.4","What is deadweight welfare loss?","The loss of total surplus (CS + PS) when output isn't at the efficient level."],
["mi2.4","Where is total surplus maximised?","At the free-market equilibrium in a competitive market with no market failure."],

/* 2.5 Interaction of markets */
["mi2.5","Define equilibrium price.","The price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, so there's no tendency for price to change."],
["mi2.5","What is excess demand and what happens?","Quantity demanded > quantity supplied at the current price. Shortages put upward pressure on price until equilibrium is restored."],
["mi2.5","What is excess supply and what happens?","Quantity supplied > quantity demanded. Unsold stock builds up, so firms cut prices."],
["mi2.5","What does ceteris paribus mean?","‘All other things being equal’: we change one variable and hold all others constant."],
["mi2.5","Demand rises, supply unchanged: what happens to P and Q?","Both price and quantity rise."],
["mi2.5","Supply rises, demand unchanged: what happens to P and Q?","Price falls and quantity rises."],
["mi2.5","Demand and supply both rise: what happens?","Quantity definitely rises. The effect on price depends on which shift is bigger."],

/* 2.6 Elasticity */
["mi2.6","Define elasticity.","How responsive one variable is to a change in another."],
["mi2.6","Formula for PED?","PED = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price."],
["mi2.6","What do PED values mean?","Above 1 (ignoring sign): elastic.\nBelow 1: inelastic.\nExactly 1: unitary.\n0: perfectly inelastic.\nInfinite: perfectly elastic."],
["mi2.6","Calculate: price rises 10%, quantity demanded falls 5%.","PED = −5 ÷ 10 = −0.5. Inelastic."],
["mi2.6","Calculate: price falls from £10 to £8, quantity rises from 100 to 150.","%ΔP = −20%, %ΔQ = +50%.\nPED = 50 ÷ −20 = −2.5. Elastic."],
["mi2.6","What determines PED? (SPLAT)","Substitutes (number and closeness), Proportion of income spent, Luxury or necessity, Addictive or habit-forming, Time period."],
["mi2.6","PED and total revenue: what's the rule?","Elastic demand: cut price to raise TR.\nInelastic demand: raise price to raise TR.\nUnitary: TR unchanged. TR is maximised where PED = 1."],
["mi2.6","Formula for YED?","YED = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in income."],
["mi2.6","What do YED values tell you?","Positive: normal good.\nAbove 1: luxury (income elastic).\nBetween 0 and 1: necessity.\nNegative: inferior good."],
["mi2.6","Give an example of an inferior good.","Supermarket own-brand food, bus travel: demand falls as incomes rise because people switch to better alternatives."],
["mi2.6","Formula for XED?","XED = % change in quantity demanded of good A ÷ % change in price of good B."],
["mi2.6","What do XED values tell you?","Positive: substitutes.\nNegative: complements.\nAround zero: unrelated goods.\nThe bigger the number, the closer the relationship."],
["mi2.6","Formula for PES?","PES = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price."],
["mi2.6","What determines PES?","Spare capacity, stock levels, time period, how easily factors can be switched between uses, length of the production process."],
["mi2.6","Why is supply of agricultural goods inelastic in the short run?","Crops take months to grow, so output can't respond quickly to a price change."],
["mi2.6","How does PED affect who pays an indirect tax?","The more inelastic demand is, the more of the tax is passed on to consumers through higher prices."],
["mi2.6","Why do governments tax goods with inelastic demand?","Consumption falls little, so the tax raises lots of revenue. But it does little to change behaviour."],
["mi2.6","Calculate YED: income rises 4%, demand rises 8%.","YED = 8 ÷ 4 = +2. Normal, luxury good."],

/* 2.7 The margin */
["mi2.7","What does ‘the margin’ mean?","The effect of one more (or one fewer) unit: the extra cost, benefit or revenue of the last unit."],
["mi2.7","Define total utility.","The total satisfaction gained from consuming a quantity of a good."],
["mi2.7","Define marginal utility.","The extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit of a good."],
["mi2.7","State the law of diminishing marginal utility.","As more units of a good are consumed, the extra satisfaction from each additional unit falls."],
["mi2.7","How does diminishing marginal utility explain the demand curve?","Each extra unit is worth less to the consumer, so they're only willing to buy more if the price falls."],
["mi2.7","Calculate: total cost is £100 for 10 units and £112 for 11.","Marginal cost of the 11th unit = £112 − £100 = £12."],
["mi2.7","When is total utility at its maximum?","When marginal utility is zero."],

/* 2.8 Market failure and externalities */
["mi2.8","Define market failure.","When the free market allocates resources inefficiently, so social welfare isn't maximised."],
["mi2.8","Define an externality.","A cost or benefit that affects a third party not involved in the transaction, and isn't reflected in the price."],
["mi2.8","MSC formula?","Marginal social cost = marginal private cost + marginal external cost."],
["mi2.8","MSB formula?","Marginal social benefit = marginal private benefit + marginal external benefit."],
["mi2.8","Where is the social optimum output?","Where MSC = MSB."],
["mi2.8","Example of a negative production externality?","A factory polluting a river, which harms fishermen downstream."],
["mi2.8","Example of a negative consumption externality?","Passive smoking; noise from loud music; congestion from driving."],
["mi2.8","Example of a positive production externality?","A firm training workers who later work for other firms; a beekeeper's bees pollinating nearby farms."],
["mi2.8","Example of a positive consumption externality?","Vaccination (protects others too); education (a more productive workforce, lower crime)."],
["mi2.8","Is a good with a negative externality over- or under-produced?","Overproduced and underpriced: the market ignores the external cost."],
["mi2.8","Is a good with a positive externality over- or under-consumed?","Underconsumed: the market ignores the external benefit."],
["mi2.8","List the main types of market failure.","Externalities, public goods, information failure (merit and demerit goods), monopoly power, factor immobility, inequality."],
["mi2.8","What is the welfare loss triangle?","The area between MSC and MSB over the units produced beyond (or short of) the social optimum."],
["mi2.8","Complete vs partial market failure?","Complete: the market doesn't provide the good at all (a missing market, e.g. public goods).\nPartial: the market provides it, but in the wrong quantity or at the wrong price."],

/* 2.9 Information failure */
["mi2.9","Define information failure.","When economic agents have incomplete or inaccurate information, so they make decisions that don't maximise their welfare."],
["mi2.9","Define asymmetric information.","When one party in a transaction has more information than the other, e.g. a used-car seller knows more about the car than the buyer."],
["mi2.9","Define moral hazard.","When someone takes more risk because they don't bear the full cost of it, e.g. an insured driver driving carelessly; banks believing they're too big to fail."],
["mi2.9","Define a merit good.","A good that's underconsumed in a free market because people underestimate its private benefits (and it often has positive externalities). Examples: education, healthcare, pensions."],
["mi2.9","Define a demerit good.","A good that's overconsumed because people underestimate its harm to themselves (and it often has negative externalities). Examples: cigarettes, alcohol, gambling."],
["mi2.9","Policies to increase consumption of merit goods?","Free state provision, subsidies, information campaigns, regulation (compulsory schooling)."],
["mi2.9","Policies to reduce consumption of demerit goods?","Indirect taxes, bans, age restrictions, advertising bans, plain packaging, information and health warnings."],
["mi2.9","Why is the ‘merit good’ label controversial?","It's paternalistic: the government decides what's good for people, which is a value judgement."],

/* 2.10 Public goods */
["mi2.10","Define a public good.","A good that is non-excludable and non-rival, so the free market won't provide it. Examples: street lighting, national defence, lighthouses."],
["mi2.10","Define non-excludability.","Once the good is provided, nobody can be stopped from benefiting, even if they don't pay."],
["mi2.10","Define non-rivalry (non-diminishability).","One person's consumption doesn't reduce the amount available to others."],
["mi2.10","Define non-rejectability.","People can't opt out of consuming the good, e.g. national defence."],
["mi2.10","Why does a public good have zero marginal cost?","Providing it to one extra person costs nothing more."],
["mi2.10","What is the free rider problem?","People benefit without paying because they can't be excluded, so no private firm can make a profit providing the good. It leads to a missing market."],
["mi2.10","Define a quasi-public good.","A good with some, but not all, characteristics of a public good, e.g. roads (can be tolled; congestion makes them rival)."],
["mi2.10","Define a private good.","A good that is excludable and rival, e.g. a sandwich."],
["mi2.10","How can technology change whether a good is public?","It can make goods excludable, e.g. road tolls with number-plate cameras, or encrypted TV."],

/* 2.11 Government intervention */
["mi2.11","Specific vs ad valorem indirect tax?","Specific: a fixed amount per unit (fuel duty). Supply shifts up in parallel.\nAd valorem: a percentage of the price (VAT). Supply pivots upwards."],
["mi2.11","How does an indirect tax correct a negative externality?","By setting the tax equal to the marginal external cost, MPC rises to MSC, so output falls to the social optimum. The polluter pays."],
["mi2.11","How does a subsidy correct a positive externality?","It lowers costs or prices, so output or consumption rises towards the social optimum where MSB = MSC."],
["mi2.11","What is a maximum price and what does it cause?","A legal price ceiling set below equilibrium. It causes excess demand, shortages, queues and black markets. Example: rent controls."],
["mi2.11","What is a minimum price and what does it cause?","A legal price floor set above equilibrium. It causes excess supply. Example: minimum unit pricing for alcohol in Scotland; the minimum wage."],
["mi2.11","What is a buffer stock scheme?","An agency buys up a commodity when its price is low and sells stock when its price is high, to keep the price within a band."],
["mi2.11","Problems with buffer stock schemes?","High storage costs, perishable goods, and the agency runs out of money if it sets the target price too high."],
["mi2.11","What is a public-private partnership (PPP)?","Private firms finance, build and often run public projects (hospitals, schools), and the government pays them over many years."],
["mi2.11","How do tradable pollution permits work?","The government caps total pollution and issues permits. Firms that can cut pollution cheaply sell their spare permits to those that can't, so pollution is cut at the lowest cost."],
["mi2.11","Legislation vs regulation?","Legislation: laws passed by Parliament (e.g. banning smoking indoors).\nRegulation: rules enforced by regulators such as Ofwat or Ofgem."],
["mi2.11","Define government failure.","When government intervention makes resource allocation worse, causing a net welfare loss."],
["mi2.11","Causes of government failure?","Poor information, conflicting objectives, administrative costs, unintended consequences, political self-interest, regulatory capture."],
["mi2.11","What is regulatory capture?","When a regulator ends up acting in the interests of the firms it regulates rather than the public."],
["mi2.11","Example of an unintended consequence of intervention?","High cigarette taxes encouraging smuggling; rent controls reducing the supply of rented housing."],
["mi2.11","What is competition policy?","Government action to promote competition: blocking anti-competitive mergers, breaking up cartels, controlling abuse of monopoly power. In the UK, run by the CMA."],

/* 3.1 Business objectives */
["mi3.1","Condition for profit maximisation?","MC = MR (with MC rising)."],
["mi3.1","Condition for revenue maximisation?","MR = 0."],
["mi3.1","Condition for sales volume maximisation?","AR = AC: the highest output without making a loss (normal profit)."],
["mi3.1","Define profit satisficing.","Aiming for a satisfactory level of profit rather than the maximum, often to keep shareholders content while managers pursue other goals."],
["mi3.1","Define corporate social responsibility (CSR).","When a firm takes into account the effects of its actions on all stakeholders and the environment, not just its profit."],
["mi3.1","Define the principal-agent problem.","When the agent (e.g. managers) has different objectives from the principal (e.g. shareholders) and the principal can't fully monitor them."],
["mi3.1","Why might managers pursue growth rather than profit?","Bigger firms bring higher salaries, status and power for managers, plus economies of scale and protection from takeover."],
["mi3.1","Why might a firm maximise profit in the long run but not the short run?","It may cut prices to gain market share or deter entrants (limit pricing), sacrificing profit now for more later."],
["mi3.1","Why is utility maximisation relevant to firms?","Owners or managers may run the firm to maximise their own satisfaction, e.g. a family firm valuing independence or a lifestyle business."],

/* 3.2 Costs and economies of scale */
["mi3.2","Define fixed costs.","Costs that don't change with output in the short run, e.g. rent, insurance, interest on loans."],
["mi3.2","Define variable costs.","Costs that change with output, e.g. raw materials, energy, wages of hourly staff."],
["mi3.2","Formulas: TC, AC, AFC, AVC?","TC = FC + VC\nAC = TC ÷ Q\nAFC = FC ÷ Q\nAVC = VC ÷ Q\nAC = AFC + AVC"],
["mi3.2","Formula for marginal cost?","MC = change in total cost ÷ change in output."],
["mi3.2","Calculate: TC = £500 at 50 units. What is AC?","AC = 500 ÷ 50 = £10."],
["mi3.2","Short run vs long run?","Short run: at least one factor of production is fixed.\nLong run: all factors are variable."],
["mi3.2","State the law of diminishing returns.","In the short run, as more of a variable factor is added to a fixed factor, the marginal product of the variable factor eventually falls."],
["mi3.2","Why is the SRAC curve U-shaped?","Falling AFC and increasing returns at first, then rising MC from diminishing returns pushes AC up."],
["mi3.2","Why is the LRAC curve U-shaped?","Economies of scale make it fall, then diseconomies of scale make it rise."],
["mi3.2","Define economies of scale.","The fall in long-run average cost as a firm increases its scale of production."],
["mi3.2","List the types of internal economies of scale.","Technical, purchasing (bulk buying), managerial, financial, marketing, risk-bearing."],
["mi3.2","Explain technical economies of scale.","Larger firms can use bigger, more efficient machinery, and a bigger container holds more per unit of material (container ships, the law of increased dimensions)."],
["mi3.2","Explain financial economies of scale.","Larger firms are seen as less risky, so they can borrow more cheaply."],
["mi3.2","Define external economies of scale.","Falling average costs for all firms because the whole industry grows, e.g. a skilled local labour pool, specialist suppliers nearby, shared infrastructure."],
["mi3.2","Define diseconomies of scale.","The rise in long-run average cost when a firm grows too large."],
["mi3.2","Causes of diseconomies of scale?","Poor communication, coordination problems, loss of control, low worker motivation and alienation."],
["mi3.2","Define minimum efficient scale (MES).","The lowest level of output at which long-run average cost is minimised."],
["mi3.2","Why does a high MES matter?","Only a few firms can produce at the lowest cost, so it's a barrier to entry and leads to oligopoly or natural monopoly."],

/* 3.3 Revenue and profit */
["mi3.3","Formulas: TR, AR, MR?","TR = P × Q\nAR = TR ÷ Q (= price)\nMR = change in TR ÷ change in Q"],
["mi3.3","Formula for profit?","Profit = total revenue − total cost."],
["mi3.3","Define normal profit.","The minimum profit needed to keep a firm in its current industry. It counts as a cost (the opportunity cost of the entrepreneur). When AR = AC, the firm makes normal profit."],
["mi3.3","Define supernormal profit.","Profit above normal profit, earned when AR > AC. It signals other firms to enter."],
["mi3.3","Define accounting profit.","Total revenue minus explicit (paid-out) costs. It ignores opportunity costs."],
["mi3.3","When does a firm shut down in the short run?","When price falls below average variable cost (P < AVC)."],
["mi3.3","When does a firm leave the industry in the long run?","When price is below average cost (P < AC), so it makes a loss it can't sustain."],
["mi3.3","Calculate: price £5, output 100, total cost £400.","TR = £500. Profit = £500 − £400 = £100."],

/* 4.1 Perfect competition */
["mi4.1","Characteristics of perfect competition?","Many buyers and sellers; identical (homogeneous) products; perfect information; no barriers to entry or exit; firms are price takers."],
["mi4.1","What does a perfectly competitive firm's demand curve look like?","Horizontal (perfectly elastic) at the market price: P = AR = MR."],
["mi4.1","Long-run equilibrium in perfect competition?","P = AR = MR = MC = AC. Firms earn normal profit and are productively and allocatively efficient."],
["mi4.1","Why does supernormal profit disappear in the long run?","Free entry: new firms join, supply shifts right, price falls to minimum AC."],
["mi4.1","What is the firm's supply curve in perfect competition?","The MC curve above AVC."],
["mi4.1","Give real-world examples close to perfect competition.","Agricultural markets (wheat), foreign exchange markets, some online markets."],
["mi4.1","Weakness of perfect competition?","No supernormal profit to fund R&D, so possibly little dynamic efficiency; no economies of scale; no product variety."],

/* 4.2 Monopoly */
["mi4.2","Define a pure monopoly.","A single firm supplying the entire market."],
["mi4.2","What market share counts as a monopoly under UK law?","25% or more."],
["mi4.2","List barriers to entry.","Economies of scale; legal barriers (patents, licences); control of resources; brand loyalty; high sunk costs; predatory pricing."],
["mi4.2","Define dynamic efficiency.","Improvements in efficiency over time through innovation and investment in new products and processes."],
["mi4.2","Define X-inefficiency.","When a firm's costs are higher than they need to be because a lack of competition means there's little pressure to control them."],
["mi4.2","Define price discrimination.","Charging different prices to different consumers for the same good, for reasons unrelated to cost."],
["mi4.2","Conditions for price discrimination?","The firm has market power; markets can be separated with no resale between them; different groups have different PEDs."],
["mi4.2","Examples of price discrimination?","Peak and off-peak train fares; student and pensioner discounts; airline seats priced by booking time."],
["mi4.2","Define natural monopoly.","An industry where economies of scale are so large that one firm can supply the whole market at lower cost than two or more firms could. Example: water pipes, rail track."],
["mi4.2","Why is a monopoly allocatively inefficient?","It produces where MC = MR and charges a price above MC (P > MC), so output is lower than the social optimum."],
["mi4.2","Arguments in favour of monopoly?","Economies of scale lowering costs; supernormal profit funding R&D (dynamic efficiency); able to compete internationally; avoids wasteful duplication (natural monopoly)."],

/* 4.3 Monopolistic competition */
["mi4.3","Characteristics of monopolistic competition?","Many firms; slightly differentiated products; low barriers to entry and exit; some price-setting power."],
["mi4.3","Examples of monopolistic competition?","Restaurants, hairdressers, clothing shops, plumbers."],
["mi4.3","Long-run outcome in monopolistic competition?","Normal profit, with AR tangent to AC. Not allocatively or productively efficient."],
["mi4.3","What is excess capacity?","The firm produces below the output that minimises AC, so it could produce more at lower cost."],

/* 4.4 Oligopoly */
["mi4.4","Characteristics of oligopoly?","A few large firms dominate; high barriers to entry; interdependence; differentiated or identical products; non-price competition."],
["mi4.4","Define interdependence.","Each firm's decisions depend on how it expects rivals to react."],
["mi4.4","Explain the kinked demand curve.","If a firm raises its price, rivals don't follow, so demand is elastic and it loses sales. If it cuts price, rivals follow, so demand is inelastic and it gains little. Result: price rigidity."],
["mi4.4","Overt vs tacit collusion?","Overt: a formal agreement to fix prices or output (a cartel; illegal in the UK).\nTacit: no formal agreement, e.g. following a price leader."],
["mi4.4","Why are cartels unstable?","Each member has an incentive to cheat by cutting price or raising output to grab profit, as in the prisoners' dilemma."],
["mi4.4","Examples of non-price competition?","Advertising, branding, loyalty cards, product quality, after-sales service, packaging."],
["mi4.4","Define product differentiation.","Making a product different from rivals' through branding, design or quality, so demand becomes less price elastic."],
["mi4.4","Define the concentration ratio.","The combined market share of the largest n firms in a market."],
["mi4.4","Calculate a 3-firm concentration ratio: shares are 30%, 25%, 15%, 10%, 20% (others).","30 + 25 + 15 = 70%. A 3-firm ratio of 70% suggests oligopoly."],
["mi4.4","Example of UK oligopolies?","Supermarkets (Tesco, Sainsbury's, Asda, Aldi), mobile networks, banking, energy supply."],

/* 4.5 Contestable markets */
["mi4.5","Define a contestable market.","A market with low barriers to entry and exit, so the threat of new entrants keeps incumbents behaving competitively."],
["mi4.5","Define sunk costs.","Costs that can't be recovered on leaving the market, e.g. advertising, specialist equipment. High sunk costs reduce contestability."],
["mi4.5","What is hit-and-run competition?","Firms enter a market to take short-run supernormal profit, then leave, because exit is costless."],
["mi4.5","What does contestable market theory imply for policy?","Lowering barriers to entry matters more than the number of firms in the market."],

/* 5.1 Demand for labour */
["mi5.1","Define derived demand.","Labour is demanded for what it produces, not for itself. Demand for labour depends on demand for the product."],
["mi5.1","Formula for MRP?","MRP = marginal physical product (MPP) × marginal revenue (MR)."],
["mi5.1","How many workers does a profit-maximising firm employ?","Where the wage = MRP of the last worker."],
["mi5.1","Factors affecting demand for labour?","Wage rate; demand for the product; productivity of labour; price of capital (substitutes); number of firms."],
["mi5.1","Factors affecting the wage elasticity of demand for labour?","PED for the product; proportion of labour costs in total costs; ease of substituting capital for labour; time period."],
["mi5.1","Define labour productivity.","Output per worker (or per hour worked)."],
["mi5.1","Define unit labour costs.","Labour costs per unit of output = total labour costs ÷ output."],

/* 5.2 Supply of labour */
["mi5.2","Factors affecting the supply of labour to an industry?","Wage rate; non-monetary benefits (job satisfaction, conditions); qualifications and training needed; size of the working population; migration; trade unions."],
["mi5.2","Factors affecting the wage elasticity of supply of labour?","Qualifications and skills needed; length of training; time period; labour mobility; level of unemployment."],
["mi5.2","Define economic rent.","Earnings above transfer earnings: the surplus over the minimum needed to keep a worker in the job."],
["mi5.2","Define transfer earnings.","The minimum a worker must be paid to stay in their current job rather than move to the next best one."],
["mi5.2","Why do top footballers earn mostly economic rent?","Their supply is very inelastic (rare talent), so most of their wage is above what they'd earn in their next best job."],
["mi5.2","Short run vs long run supply of labour?","Short run: fixed by existing skills, so inelastic.\nLong run: people can train and move, so more elastic."],
["mi5.2","Occupational vs geographical immobility?","Occupational: difficulty switching jobs because of lack of skills.\nGeographical: difficulty moving location (house prices, family ties)."],

/* 5.3 Labour markets */
["mi5.3","Define wage differentials.","Differences in wages between workers, occupations, industries or regions."],
["mi5.3","Causes of wage differentials?","Differences in MRP and skills; supply of workers; non-monetary benefits; trade union power; discrimination; monopsony power; regional costs."],
["mi5.3","Define monopsony.","A single (or dominant) buyer, e.g. of labour."],
["mi5.3","Effect of monopsony on a labour market?","Lower wages and lower employment than in a competitive labour market."],
["mi5.3","Define a trade union.","An organisation of workers that bargains collectively with employers over pay and conditions."],
["mi5.3","Define bilateral monopoly.","A monopsony employer bargaining with a monopoly seller of labour (a trade union). The wage depends on bargaining power."],
["mi5.3","Effect of the national minimum wage in a competitive labour market?","If set above equilibrium, there's excess supply of labour, so unemployment rises."],
["mi5.3","Effect of a minimum wage under monopsony?","Can raise both wages and employment."],
["mi5.3","Define labour market flexibility.","How easily the labour market adjusts to change: ease of hiring and firing, flexible hours and contracts, and worker mobility."],

/* ======================= MACRO ======================= */
/* 1.1 Circular flow */
["ma1.1","What flows between households and firms in the circular flow?","Households provide factors of production; firms pay incomes. Households spend on goods and services; firms provide them."],
["ma1.1","What are the three injections?","Investment (I), government spending (G), exports (X)."],
["ma1.1","What are the three withdrawals (leakages)?","Savings (S), taxation (T), imports (M)."],
["ma1.1","When is national income in equilibrium?","When injections equal withdrawals: I + G + X = S + T + M."],
["ma1.1","Define GDP.","The total value of all goods and services produced within a country in a year."],
["ma1.1","What are the three ways of measuring national income?","Output (value added by each sector), income (wages, rent, interest, profit), expenditure (C + I + G + X − M). They should give the same total."],

/* 1.2 Aggregate demand */
["ma1.2","Define aggregate demand.","Total planned spending on an economy's goods and services at each price level over a period."],
["ma1.2","Formula for AD?","AD = C + I + G + (X − M)."],
["ma1.2","What is the largest component of UK AD?","Consumption, roughly 60% of GDP."],
["ma1.2","Determinants of consumption?","Disposable income, interest rates, consumer confidence, wealth (house prices), availability of credit, expectations."],
["ma1.2","Determinants of investment?","Interest rates, business confidence, expected demand (the accelerator), technology, corporation tax, availability of finance."],
["ma1.2","Determinants of net exports?","Exchange rate, incomes abroad, relative inflation, quality and competitiveness, trade barriers."],
["ma1.2","Why does AD slope downwards?","A lower price level gives: a wealth effect (real wealth rises), a trade effect (exports more competitive), and an interest rate effect (rates tend to fall)."],
["ma1.2","What happens to AD if the Bank of England raises interest rates?","AD shifts left: borrowing is dearer and saving more attractive, so C and I fall; a stronger pound reduces net exports."],

/* 1.3 Aggregate supply */
["ma1.3","Define aggregate supply.","The total amount of goods and services firms are willing and able to produce at each price level over a period."],
["ma1.3","Why does SRAS slope upwards?","Some costs (like wages) are fixed in the short run, so a higher price level raises profitability and firms produce more."],
["ma1.3","Why is classical LRAS vertical?","In the long run, output depends on the quantity and quality of factors of production, not on the price level."],
["ma1.3","What shifts SRAS?","Changes in costs: wages, raw material and energy prices, indirect taxes, subsidies, the exchange rate (import costs), short-run productivity."],
["ma1.3","What shifts LRAS?","Changes in the quantity or quality of factors of production: technology, education and training, investment, population and migration, institutions, supply-side policies."],

/* 1.4 Interaction of AD and AS */
["ma1.4","Where is macroeconomic equilibrium?","Where AD = AS: it sets the equilibrium price level and real output."],
["ma1.4","Effect of an increase in AD (with spare capacity)?","Real GDP rises, unemployment falls, the price level rises a little."],
["ma1.4","Effect of a negative supply shock?","SRAS shifts left: the price level rises and real GDP falls (stagflation)."],
["ma1.4","Demand-side vs supply-side shock?","Demand-side: a sudden change in AD (e.g. a financial crisis cutting spending).\nSupply-side: a sudden change in AS (e.g. an oil price spike)."],
["ma1.4","Key assumptions of the AD/AS model?","Ceteris paribus; aggregate behaviour can be modelled like a single market; full employment output is fixed in the short run."],

/* 1.5 Multiplier and accelerator */
["ma1.5","Define the multiplier.","The process by which an initial change in an injection leads to a larger final change in national income."],
["ma1.5","Formula for the multiplier?","k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPW, where MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM."],
["ma1.5","Calculate: MPC = 0.8 (closed economy, no tax). Government spends an extra £10bn.","k = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.8) = 5. National income rises by £10bn × 5 = £50bn."],
["ma1.5","Calculate: MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.2, MPM = 0.2.","MPW = 0.5, so k = 1 ÷ 0.5 = 2."],
["ma1.5","Define marginal propensity to consume (MPC).","The proportion of an extra pound of income that is spent: ΔC ÷ ΔY."],
["ma1.5","Define average propensity to consume (APC).","The proportion of total income that is spent: C ÷ Y."],
["ma1.5","Define marginal propensity to save (MPS).","The proportion of an extra pound of income that is saved: ΔS ÷ ΔY."],
["ma1.5","What is MPW?","The marginal propensity to withdraw: the share of extra income that leaks out = MPS + MPT + MPM."],
["ma1.5","How are MPC and MPW related?","MPC + MPW = 1 (each extra pound is either spent on domestic output or withdrawn)."],
["ma1.5","What determines the size of the multiplier?","The size of withdrawals: higher savings, tax rates or import spending mean a smaller multiplier."],
["ma1.5","Why is the UK multiplier relatively small?","High imports (open economy) and relatively high tax rates mean large withdrawals."],
["ma1.5","Define the accelerator.","Investment depends on the rate of change of national income: when GDP growth speeds up, investment rises by proportionally more."],
["ma1.5","Why is investment more volatile than GDP?","Because of the accelerator: a small change in the growth rate of output causes a large change in the investment needed."],
["ma1.5","How do the multiplier and accelerator interact?","Rising investment raises income via the multiplier; rising income raises investment via the accelerator. This amplifies booms and recessions."],
["ma1.5","Define an output gap.","The difference between actual output and potential (trend) output."],
["ma1.5","Negative vs positive output gap?","Negative: actual < potential (spare capacity, unemployment, low inflation).\nPositive: actual > potential (overheating, inflationary pressure)."],

/* 2.1 Economic growth */
["ma2.1","Define short-run economic growth.","An increase in real GDP, usually from higher AD using spare capacity."],
["ma2.1","Define long-run economic growth.","An increase in productive capacity (potential output), shown by LRAS shifting right or the PPC shifting out."],
["ma2.1","Real vs nominal GDP?","Nominal GDP is measured at current prices. Real GDP is adjusted for inflation, so it measures the actual change in output."],
["ma2.1","Formula for GDP per capita?","GDP ÷ population."],
["ma2.1","Formula for the economic growth rate?","(Real GDP this year − real GDP last year) ÷ real GDP last year × 100."],
["ma2.1","Calculate: real GDP rises from £2,000bn to £2,050bn.","Growth = 50 ÷ 2,000 × 100 = 2.5%."],
["ma2.1","What are the stages of the economic cycle?","Boom, downturn (slowdown), recession, recovery."],
["ma2.1","Define a recession.","Two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth."],
["ma2.1","Define trend growth.","The average sustainable rate of growth over a long period. Potential output grows at this rate."],
["ma2.1","Causes of long-run growth?","Investment in capital, technology and innovation, education and training, a growing labour force, efficient institutions."],
["ma2.1","Benefits of economic growth?","Higher living standards, lower unemployment, more tax revenue, better public services, reduced poverty."],
["ma2.1","Costs of economic growth?","Inflation (if demand-led), environmental damage, resource depletion, widening inequality, current account deficits."],

/* 2.2 Development */
["ma2.2","Define the primary, secondary and tertiary sectors.","Primary: extracting natural resources (farming, mining).\nSecondary: manufacturing and construction.\nTertiary: services (finance, retail, healthcare)."],
["ma2.2","How does a country's sector structure change as it develops?","Employment shifts from primary to secondary, then to tertiary. The UK is now mostly services (about 80% of output)."],
["ma2.2","Define sustainable development.","Development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs (the Brundtland definition)."],
["ma2.2","Economic growth vs economic development?","Growth: an increase in real GDP.\nDevelopment: a wider improvement in living standards and welfare (health, education, freedom)."],
["ma2.2","What does the HDI measure?","Three dimensions: health (life expectancy), education (expected and mean years of schooling) and income (GNI per capita at PPP). Scored from 0 to 1."],
["ma2.2","Limitations of GDP as a measure of living standards?","Ignores distribution, the informal economy, unpaid work, environmental damage, leisure and quality of goods; exchange rate issues when comparing countries."],
["ma2.2","Give alternative measures of welfare.","HDI, Gini coefficient, happiness and wellbeing surveys (ONS), Genuine Progress Indicator, Multidimensional Poverty Index."],

/* 2.3 Employment */
["ma2.3","How is someone defined as unemployed (ILO)?","Without a job, available to start within two weeks, and actively looked for work in the past four weeks."],
["ma2.3","What is the Labour Force Survey?","A quarterly household survey of about 40,000 households using the ILO definition. It's the official UK unemployment measure."],
["ma2.3","What is the claimant count?","The number of people claiming unemployment-related benefits. It usually gives a lower figure than the LFS."],
["ma2.3","Formula for the unemployment rate?","Number unemployed ÷ labour force (employed + unemployed) × 100."],
["ma2.3","Define cyclical unemployment.","Unemployment caused by a lack of aggregate demand in a downturn or recession. Also called demand-deficient unemployment."],
["ma2.3","Define structural unemployment.","Unemployment caused by a mismatch between workers' skills or location and the jobs available, e.g. after the decline of coal mining."],
["ma2.3","Define frictional unemployment.","Short-term unemployment while people move between jobs."],
["ma2.3","Define seasonal unemployment.","Unemployment caused by seasonal changes in demand, e.g. tourism, farming."],
["ma2.3","Define real-wage unemployment.","Unemployment caused by wages being held above equilibrium, e.g. by a minimum wage or trade unions."],
["ma2.3","Define full employment.","When everyone who wants a job at the going wage has one. In practice some frictional unemployment remains."],
["ma2.3","Define hysteresis.","When unemployment persists because the long-term unemployed lose skills and motivation and become less employable."],
["ma2.3","Costs of unemployment?","To individuals: lost income, poorer health. To government: lower tax revenue, higher benefits. To the economy: lost output (inside the PPC). To society: crime, social problems."],
["ma2.3","Define underemployment.","People in work who want more hours than they have, or who are in jobs below their skill level."],
["ma2.3","Define economic inactivity.","People of working age who are neither employed nor unemployed, e.g. students, carers, the long-term sick."],

/* 2.4 Inflation */
["ma2.4","Define inflation.","A sustained rise in the general price level."],
["ma2.4","Define deflation.","A sustained fall in the general price level (negative inflation)."],
["ma2.4","Define disinflation.","A fall in the rate of inflation: prices still rise, but more slowly."],
["ma2.4","Define hyperinflation.","Extremely high and accelerating inflation, e.g. above 50% a month. Examples: Zimbabwe (2008), Germany (1923)."],
["ma2.4","What is the UK inflation target?","2% CPI inflation, set by the government for the Bank of England."],
["ma2.4","How is CPI calculated?","A survey of household spending sets weights; prices of a basket of around 700 goods and services are collected monthly; a weighted price index is calculated."],
["ma2.4","CPI vs RPI?","RPI includes housing costs like mortgage interest payments and council tax; CPI doesn't. RPI uses a different averaging formula and is usually higher."],
["ma2.4","Calculate the inflation rate: the index rises from 120 to 126.","(126 − 120) ÷ 120 × 100 = 5%."],
["ma2.4","How do you convert a nominal value to a real value?","Real value = nominal value ÷ price index × 100 (using the base year index = 100)."],
["ma2.4","Calculate: nominal wages rise 6%, inflation is 4%.","Real wages rise by roughly 2%."],
["ma2.4","Define demand-pull inflation.","Inflation caused by AD growing faster than the economy's capacity: too much money chasing too few goods."],
["ma2.4","Define cost-push inflation.","Inflation caused by rising costs of production (wages, energy, raw materials, a weaker pound), shifting SRAS left."],
["ma2.4","Costs of inflation?","Menu costs, shoe-leather costs, falling real incomes for those on fixed incomes, redistribution from savers to borrowers, lost international competitiveness, uncertainty discouraging investment."],
["ma2.4","Costs of deflation?","Consumers delay spending, real debt burdens rise, wages are hard to cut so unemployment rises, risk of a deflationary spiral."],
["ma2.4","What is ‘good’ deflation?","Falling prices caused by productivity gains or lower costs (SRAS or LRAS shifting right), with output rising."],
["ma2.4","What is the wage-price spiral?","Higher prices lead workers to demand higher wages, which raise firms' costs and prices, and so on."],

/* 2.5 Balance of payments */
["ma2.5","Define the balance of payments.","A record of all financial transactions between a country and the rest of the world over a period."],
["ma2.5","What are the main accounts of the balance of payments?","Current account, capital account, financial account."],
["ma2.5","What is trade in goods?","Exports minus imports of physical goods (visible trade)."],
["ma2.5","What is trade in services?","Exports minus imports of services, such as finance, tourism and insurance (invisible trade). The UK has a surplus."],
["ma2.5","What is primary income?","Income from investments abroad (profits, interest, dividends) and wages earned abroad, minus the same flowing out."],
["ma2.5","What is secondary income?","Transfers with nothing given in return: foreign aid, remittances from migrant workers, payments to international organisations."],
["ma2.5","What is the financial account?","Flows of investment: FDI, portfolio investment (shares and bonds), bank flows and reserves."],
["ma2.5","Causes of a current account deficit?","Strong consumer demand sucking in imports, low competitiveness, a strong exchange rate, high inflation, weak growth abroad."],
["ma2.5","Expenditure-switching vs expenditure-reducing policies?","Switching: make domestic goods relatively cheaper (devaluation, tariffs).\nReducing: cut total spending (higher taxes, higher interest rates) so imports fall."],
["ma2.5","Calculate the current account: goods −£200bn, services +£150bn, primary −£20bn, secondary −£25bn.","−200 + 150 − 20 − 25 = −£95bn: a current account deficit."],
["ma2.5","Why does the overall balance of payments always balance?","A current account deficit must be financed by a surplus on the financial and capital accounts (e.g. foreign investment inflows)."],

/* 2.6 Trends */
["ma2.6","What is the UK productivity puzzle?","Since the 2008 financial crisis, UK productivity growth has been very weak compared with before 2008 and with other countries."],
["ma2.6","What happened to UK CPI inflation in 2022?","It peaked at 11.1% in October 2022, driven by energy and food prices after Russia's invasion of Ukraine."],
["ma2.6","What has the UK's current account usually looked like in the last 20 years?","A persistent deficit, financed by inflows on the financial account."],
["ma2.6","What happened to UK GDP in 2020?","It fell by about 10% because of COVID-19 lockdowns, the largest annual fall in over 300 years, followed by a strong rebound in 2021."],
["ma2.6","Where should you find up-to-date UK data?","ONS (GDP, CPI, unemployment), Bank of England (Bank Rate), OBR (public finances), OECD and IMF (international comparisons)."],

/* 2.7 Income distribution */
["ma2.7","Income vs wealth?","Income: a flow of money over time (wages, rent, interest, profit, benefits).\nWealth: a stock of assets (property, shares, savings)."],
["ma2.7","Define absolute poverty.","Lacking the income needed for basic needs (food, shelter, clothing). The World Bank line is about $2.15 a day."],
["ma2.7","Define relative poverty.","Having income well below the average in a society. The UK definition is household income below 60% of the median."],
["ma2.7","Define the Gini coefficient.","A measure of inequality from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (one person has all income). Calculated as area A ÷ (A + B) on a Lorenz curve diagram."],
["ma2.7","What does a Lorenz curve show?","The cumulative share of income received by cumulative shares of the population. The further from the line of equality, the more unequal."],
["ma2.7","What are quantiles?","Divisions of the population into equal groups: quintiles (fifths), deciles (tenths), percentiles."],
["ma2.7","Causes of income inequality?","Differences in skills and education, wealth inheritance, unemployment, regional differences, discrimination, technology favouring skilled workers, regressive taxes."],
["ma2.7","Consequences of inequality?","Poorer health and education outcomes, lower social mobility, social tension and crime; may reduce AD (the poor have higher MPCs). Some inequality creates incentives."],
["ma2.7","Policies to reduce inequality?","Progressive taxes, means-tested benefits, minimum wage, free education and healthcare, inheritance tax."],

/* 2.8 Phillips curve */
["ma2.8","Define the natural rate of unemployment.","The rate of unemployment when the labour market is in equilibrium: frictional plus structural unemployment."],
["ma2.8","What is the NAIRU?","The non-accelerating inflation rate of unemployment: the lowest unemployment rate consistent with stable inflation."],
["ma2.8","What does the short-run Phillips curve show?","An inverse relationship between unemployment and inflation: lower unemployment means higher inflation."],
["ma2.8","What does the long-run Phillips curve show?","It's vertical at the natural rate (NAIRU): no long-run trade-off between inflation and unemployment."],
["ma2.8","Why does the SRPC shift up?","Workers come to expect higher inflation and demand higher wages, so each unemployment rate comes with higher inflation (expectations-augmented)."],
["ma2.8","Define stagflation.","High inflation together with high unemployment and stagnant growth, e.g. the 1970s oil shocks."],
["ma2.8","Keynesian AS shape?","Horizontal at low output (spare capacity), then curving upward, then vertical at full employment."],
["ma2.8","Neo-classical view of AS?","SRAS slopes up; LRAS is vertical at full employment. The economy self-corrects to long-run equilibrium."],
["ma2.8","Policy implication of a vertical LRPC?","Demand-side policy can't permanently reduce unemployment below the NAIRU; only supply-side policy can lower the natural rate."],

/* 3.1 Fiscal policy */
["ma3.1","Define fiscal policy.","The use of government spending and taxation to influence the economy."],
["ma3.1","Define a direct tax.","A tax on income or wealth, paid directly to the government, e.g. income tax, corporation tax."],
["ma3.1","Define an indirect tax.","A tax on spending, collected by the seller, e.g. VAT, fuel duty."],
["ma3.1","Define a progressive tax.","The proportion of income paid in tax rises as income rises, e.g. UK income tax."],
["ma3.1","Define a proportional tax.","The same proportion of income is paid in tax at every income level (a flat tax)."],
["ma3.1","Define a regressive tax.","The proportion of income paid in tax falls as income rises, e.g. VAT and tobacco duty, measured against income."],
["ma3.1","Current vs capital government spending?","Current: day-to-day spending (NHS salaries, benefits).\nCapital: investment in long-term assets (roads, hospitals, schools)."],
["ma3.1","Define a budget deficit.","When government spending is greater than tax revenue in a year."],
["ma3.1","Define a budget surplus.","When tax revenue is greater than government spending in a year."],
["ma3.1","Cyclical vs structural deficit?","Cyclical: caused by the economic cycle; disappears when the economy recovers.\nStructural: remains even when the economy is at full capacity."],
["ma3.1","Deficit vs national debt?","Deficit: the yearly shortfall (a flow).\nNational debt: the total accumulated borrowing (a stock)."],
["ma3.1","Define automatic stabilisers.","Changes in tax revenue and spending that happen automatically over the cycle. In a recession, tax revenue falls and benefit spending rises, cushioning AD."],
["ma3.1","Define discretionary fiscal policy.","Deliberate changes in spending or tax rates by the government, e.g. a stimulus package."],
["ma3.1","Define crowding out.","When higher government borrowing raises interest rates or uses up resources, reducing private sector spending and investment."],
["ma3.1","What does the Laffer curve show?","As tax rates rise, revenue first rises, then falls, because very high rates reduce the incentive to work and increase avoidance."],
["ma3.1","Average vs marginal tax rate?","Average: total tax paid ÷ total income.\nMarginal: tax paid on the last (extra) pound earned."],
["ma3.1","Calculate: income £40,000, tax paid £6,000.","Average tax rate = 6,000 ÷ 40,000 = 15%."],
["ma3.1","Expansionary vs contractionary fiscal policy?","Expansionary: raise spending or cut taxes, so AD shifts right.\nContractionary: cut spending or raise taxes, so AD shifts left."],
["ma3.1","Limitations of fiscal policy?","Time lags, crowding out, rising debt, uncertain multiplier size, political pressures, information problems."],

/* 3.2 Monetary policy */
["ma3.2","Define monetary policy.","The use of interest rates, the money supply and the exchange rate to influence the economy. In the UK, run by the Bank of England."],
["ma3.2","Who sets UK interest rates?","The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) of the Bank of England: nine members who meet about eight times a year."],
["ma3.2","What happens if UK inflation misses the target by more than 1 percentage point?","The Governor must write an open letter to the Chancellor explaining why and what will be done."],
["ma3.2","How does a cut in Bank Rate affect the economy?","Borrowing gets cheaper and saving less attractive, mortgage payments fall, asset prices rise, the pound weakens: C, I and net exports rise, so AD rises."],
["ma3.2","Define quantitative easing (QE).","The central bank creates new money electronically to buy assets (mainly government bonds), lowering long-term interest rates and boosting spending."],
["ma3.2","When is QE used?","When interest rates are close to zero and can't be cut further, e.g. the UK from 2009 and in 2020."],
["ma3.2","Limitations of monetary policy?","Time lags (18 to 24 months), ineffective against cost-push inflation, the liquidity trap, low confidence, effects on savers and inequality."],
["ma3.2","How long do interest rate changes take to work?","Around 18 to 24 months for the full effect on inflation."],
["ma3.2","What does SPICED stand for?","Strong Pound, Imports Cheap, Exports Dear."],
["ma3.2","What is quantitative tightening (QT)?","Reversing QE: the central bank sells bonds or doesn't replace maturing ones, reducing the money supply."],

/* 3.3 Supply-side policy */
["ma3.3","Define supply-side policies.","Policies that aim to increase the economy's productive capacity, shifting LRAS right."],
["ma3.3","Market-based vs interventionist supply-side policies?","Market-based: cutting taxes, privatisation, deregulation, reducing union power.\nInterventionist: government spending on education, training, infrastructure, R&D."],
["ma3.3","Define privatisation.","Transferring ownership of a firm or industry from the public sector to the private sector, e.g. BT, British Gas, Royal Mail."],
["ma3.3","Define deregulation.","Removing rules and restrictions that limit competition, e.g. allowing private bus operators to compete for routes."],
["ma3.3","Benefits of supply-side policies?","Growth without inflation, lower structural unemployment, better international competitiveness, helps the current account."],
["ma3.3","Drawbacks of supply-side policies?","Long time lags, expensive, uncertain results, market-based policies can widen inequality."],
["ma3.3","How can tax and benefit reform increase labour supply?","Lower income tax increases the reward for working; tapering benefits gradually reduces the poverty trap."],

/* 3.4 Policy conflicts */
["ma3.4","What is the conflict between growth and inflation?","Faster demand-led growth can push the economy beyond capacity, raising inflation."],
["ma3.4","What is the conflict between growth and the current account?","As incomes rise, spending on imports rises, worsening the current account."],
["ma3.4","What is the conflict between unemployment and inflation?","The short-run Phillips curve: reducing unemployment below the NAIRU raises inflation."],
["ma3.4","What is the conflict between growth and the environment?","More output usually means more pollution and faster resource depletion."],
["ma3.4","Which type of policy can avoid most macro conflicts?","Supply-side policy, because shifting LRAS right raises output and lowers the price level at the same time."],

/* 4.1 International trade */
["ma4.1","Benefits of international trade?","Specialisation by comparative advantage, economies of scale, more choice, lower prices, competition, technology transfer."],
["ma4.1","Disadvantages of international trade?","Structural unemployment in declining industries, over-dependence on other countries, exploitation of workers, environmental costs, infant industries can't compete."],
["ma4.1","How have UK trade patterns changed?","A shift from manufacturing to services exports; China's importance has grown; after Brexit, the EU share has fallen but it's still the largest partner."],

/* 4.2 Exchange rates */
["ma4.2","Define an exchange rate.","The price of one currency in terms of another."],
["ma4.2","What is a floating exchange rate?","Determined purely by market demand for and supply of the currency, with no government intervention."],
["ma4.2","What is a fixed exchange rate?","The government or central bank sets the currency's value against another and intervenes to maintain it."],
["ma4.2","What is a managed exchange rate?","Mostly floating, but the central bank sometimes intervenes to smooth fluctuations."],
["ma4.2","Appreciation vs revaluation?","Appreciation: a rise in a floating currency's value.\nRevaluation: a deliberate rise in a fixed currency's value."],
["ma4.2","Depreciation vs devaluation?","Depreciation: a fall in a floating currency's value.\nDevaluation: a deliberate fall in a fixed currency's value."],
["ma4.2","What causes the pound to appreciate?","Higher UK interest rates (hot money inflows), rising demand for UK exports, inward investment, speculation, lower UK inflation."],
["ma4.2","Calculate: £1 = $1.25. What does a £20 UK good cost in dollars?","20 × 1.25 = $25."],
["ma4.2","Calculate: the pound falls from $1.30 to $1.20. By what % has it depreciated?","(1.20 − 1.30) ÷ 1.30 × 100 = −7.7%."],
["ma4.2","Define hot money.","Short-term capital flows that move between countries chasing the highest interest rates or expected currency gains."],
["ma4.2","Effects of a depreciation?","Exports cheaper, imports dearer, net exports rise (eventually), AD rises, inflation rises (cost-push from imports)."],

/* 4.3 Globalisation */
["ma4.3","Define globalisation.","The increasing integration of the world's economies through trade, capital flows, migration and technology."],
["ma4.3","Causes of globalisation?","Lower transport costs (containerisation), the internet and IT, trade liberalisation (WTO), growth of multinationals, financial deregulation."],
["ma4.3","Define absolute advantage.","When a country can produce more of a good than another country with the same resources."],
["ma4.3","Define comparative advantage.","When a country can produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country."],
["ma4.3","Why does comparative advantage lead to gains from trade?","If each country specialises in what it has the lowest opportunity cost for and trades, total world output rises."],
["ma4.3","Formula for the terms of trade?","Index of export prices ÷ index of import prices × 100."],
["ma4.3","Calculate: export price index 110, import price index 100.","Terms of trade = 110 ÷ 100 × 100 = 110. An improvement: each unit of exports buys more imports."],
["ma4.3","Define international competitiveness.","A country's ability to sell its goods and services in world markets."],
["ma4.3","How can you measure international competitiveness?","Relative unit labour costs, relative export prices, productivity, the Global Competitiveness Index."],
["ma4.3","State the Marshall-Lerner condition.","A depreciation improves the current account only if PED for exports + PED for imports > 1."],
["ma4.3","What does the J-curve show?","After a depreciation, the current account first gets worse (demand is inelastic in the short run) before improving as people switch."],
["ma4.3","Effects of globalisation on developing countries?","FDI, jobs, technology transfer and faster growth; but also exploitation, environmental damage and vulnerability to global shocks."],

/* 4.4 Trade policies */
["ma4.4","Define protectionism.","Policies that restrict imports to protect domestic industries."],
["ma4.4","List forms of protectionism.","Tariffs, quotas, subsidies to domestic producers, embargoes, administrative barriers (red tape, standards)."],
["ma4.4","Define a tariff.","A tax on imports."],
["ma4.4","Define a quota.","A physical limit on the quantity of a good that can be imported."],
["ma4.4","Arguments for protectionism?","Protect infant industries, protect jobs, prevent dumping, national security, correct a current account deficit, raise revenue."],
["ma4.4","Define dumping.","Selling goods abroad below their cost of production to gain market share."],
["ma4.4","Define a free trade area.","Members remove trade barriers between themselves but each keeps its own barriers against non-members, e.g. the USMCA (formerly NAFTA)."],
["ma4.4","Define a customs union.","A free trade area plus a common external tariff against non-members."],
["ma4.4","Define a common (single) market.","A customs union plus free movement of labour and capital, e.g. the EU single market."],
["ma4.4","Define monetary union.","Members share a single currency and central bank, e.g. the eurozone and the ECB."],
["ma4.4","Define economic union.","A single market plus monetary union plus harmonised economic policies (e.g. fiscal rules)."],
["ma4.4","Define trade creation.","When joining a trading bloc replaces expensive domestic production with cheaper imports from member countries."],
["ma4.4","Define trade diversion.","When joining a trading bloc shifts imports from a cheaper non-member to a more expensive member because of the common external tariff."],
["ma4.4","What does the WTO do?","Promotes free trade by negotiating cuts to trade barriers, settling trade disputes, and enforcing rules like most-favoured-nation treatment."],

/* 5.1 Money and interest rates */
["ma5.1","What are the characteristics of good money?","Durable, portable, divisible, acceptable, limited in supply, uniform, hard to counterfeit."],
["ma5.1","Define liquidity.","How easily an asset can be turned into cash without losing value. Cash is the most liquid asset."],
["ma5.1","Narrow vs broad money?","Narrow money: notes, coins and bank reserves (very liquid).\nBroad money (M4): narrow money plus bank and building society deposits."],
["ma5.1","How do commercial banks create money?","When a bank makes a loan it creates a new deposit. Only a fraction is kept as reserves, so lending multiplies the money supply."],
["ma5.1","Formula for the credit (money) multiplier?","Money multiplier = 1 ÷ reserve ratio."],
["ma5.1","Calculate: reserve ratio 10%.","Money multiplier = 1 ÷ 0.1 = 10. £1 of new reserves can support £10 of deposits."],
["ma5.1","State the Fisher equation of exchange.","MV = PT, where M = money supply, V = velocity of circulation, P = price level, T = number of transactions (or real output, Y)."],
["ma5.1","What does the quantity theory of money claim?","If V and T are constant, an increase in M leads to a proportional rise in P: inflation is caused by excess money supply."],
["ma5.1","How is the interest rate determined in the money market?","By the demand for money (liquidity preference) and the money supply. A rise in demand or a fall in supply raises the interest rate."],
["ma5.1","Why do people hold money?","Transactions motive (everyday spending), precautionary motive (emergencies), speculative motive (waiting for better investment opportunities)."],

/* 5.2 The financial sector */
["ma5.2","Roles of the financial sector?","Facilitating saving; lending to households and firms; providing a payments system; providing forward markets for currencies and commodities; providing a market for equities (shares)."],
["ma5.2","State the Harrod-Domar model.","Growth rate = savings ratio ÷ capital-output ratio. More saving leads to more investment, which leads to higher growth."],
["ma5.2","Calculate Harrod-Domar: savings ratio 20%, capital-output ratio 4.","Growth = 20 ÷ 4 = 5%."],
["ma5.2","Limitations of the Harrod-Domar model?","Assumes savings become productive investment; ignores technology, human capital and corruption; developing countries may have too little saving."],
["ma5.2","Define microfinance.","Small loans and financial services to poor people without access to traditional banks, e.g. the Grameen Bank in Bangladesh."],
["ma5.2","Why do developing countries have low saving?","Low incomes mean most income goes on necessities, plus weak banking systems and low trust in financial institutions."],

/* 5.3 Financial regulation */
["ma5.3","Main functions of the Bank of England?","Setting monetary policy; maintaining financial stability; lender of last resort; banker to the government and to commercial banks; issuing banknotes; regulating banks."],
["ma5.3","Define lender of last resort.","The central bank lends to banks in trouble when no one else will, to prevent a banking collapse."],
["ma5.3","What does the Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA) do?","Part of the Bank of England. It supervises banks, building societies and insurers to make sure they're safe and sound."],
["ma5.3","What does the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) do?","Regulates how financial firms behave towards consumers, and keeps markets fair and honest."],
["ma5.3","What does the Financial Policy Committee (FPC) do?","Part of the Bank of England. It identifies and reduces systemic risks to the whole financial system (macroprudential regulation)."],
["ma5.3","Define systemic risk.","The risk that the failure of one financial institution spreads and causes the whole financial system to collapse."],
["ma5.3","What are capital and liquidity requirements?","Rules forcing banks to hold enough capital to absorb losses (capital ratio) and enough liquid assets to meet withdrawals (liquidity ratio)."],
["ma5.3","Why are banks a source of moral hazard?","If banks believe they're too big to fail and will be bailed out, they take excessive risks."],
["ma5.3","What does the IMF do?","Monitors the world economy, lends to countries with balance of payments problems (often with conditions attached), and gives technical advice."],
["ma5.3","What does the World Bank do?","Provides long-term loans and grants to developing countries for projects that reduce poverty, e.g. infrastructure, education and health."],
["ma5.3","Criticisms of IMF conditionality?","Austerity and market reforms (structural adjustment) can deepen recessions and hurt the poor; rich countries dominate voting."]
];
